    "millsfield:images_texts": [
      "C. PARSONS DEI\nEntered according to and of Congress in the your 1850 by A. Canis in the Clade Offer of he Sisted Came of Name Vent\nCLIPPER SHIP \"NIGHTINGALE.\nGetting under weigh off the Battery. New York\nMEW YODY DIRTINET BY M CHORIES I MACO COPS\nLITHAY N CURRISE\nDEPTH OF HOLD\nTONNAGE PER\nBUILT BY S. HANSCOM IN 1851",
      "*CLIPPER TRADE MARK-REG. US PAT OFFICE - COPYRIGHT 1966 PAN AMERICAN WORLD AIRWAYS INC\nCLIPPER SHIP \"NIGHTINGALE\"\nThe front cover of this menu is a reproduction of an authentic Currier \u0026 Ives clipper ship print.\nCurrier \u0026 Ives, known as the \"Printmakers of America\", graphically preserved the glorious clipper\nship era in a magnificent series of clipper ship prints. This print, one of a series, is being reproduced\nthrough the courtesy of The Mariners Museum, Newport News, Virginia.\nThe clipper ship \"Nightingale\" was named in honor of Jenny Lind, the \"Swedish Nightingale,\"\nwhose golden voice had recently enchanted America. The \"Nightingale\" was considered one of the\nmost beautiful of all the \"American\" clippers. She was built to be exhibited at the World's Fair\nto be held in London, in 1851, as an example of the magnificent skill and craftsmanship of the\n\"Yankee\" shipbuilders. No expense was spared in her design and construction and she\nproudly\ncarried a life size carving of the \"Swedish Nightingale\" as her ship's figurehead. She started service\ncarrying passengers to and from the World's Fair in London and later was used on the Australian\nrun where she established such sailing\nrecords as going from Melbourne to New York in 75 days!\nLike Pan Am's modern Clippers* of the air, the carly clipper ships traveled the lanes of com-\nmerce to all the far places of the world ... to Japan ... to Australia ... and, in the days of the\ngreat gold rush, from America's east coast, around Cape Horn, South America, to California.\nOur current equipment... the Clipper fleet of the Twentieth Century... consists of the finest\ncommercial aircraft ever developed —aircraft that have helped us establish an unparalleled record\nfor safety, speed and service.\nLITHO IN USA -8739-0393\n2000,058.0587"
    ],
